# macOS display and audio resilience

Branch: `macos-media-recovery`. Host-only candidate; Linux, Client, transport,
authentication and codec/color behavior remain unchanged.

Observed failure classes:

- WindowServer aborted in virtual-display mode-list generation. One incident
  coincided with republishing an offline PLANK display's existing settings.
- A running desktop audio tap stopped while video/input continued. The old
  log omitted the cause; ring overrun is a plausible cause, not a proven one.
- Process-tap PCM did not follow the default output device's volume or mute.

Implementation:

1. Do not call private `applySettings` again on an existing offline display.
   Request remote-user wake and boundedly wait for the same output, preserving
   authority checks and the last successful geometry. Permanent disappearance
   remains a clear failure. No duplicate output, forced logout, reboot, global
   power change, or claim that every OS display crash is resolved.
2. Keep the lock-free audio ring bounded. Overflow rejects incoming blocks;
   the single consumer discards its stale backlog and the existing Opus clock
   handling reanchors to real timestamps. Invalid samples remain errors.
   Never overwrite consumer-owned audio or replay delayed sound.
3. Log the audio failure class and transport-send rejection. After failure of
   a previously active tap, wait for complete HAL teardown, then retry audio
   after one second, at most three times per connection. Mark the replacement
   encoder's first packet as a discontinuity. Denied/pending startup consent
   does not retry; session stop cancels pending retries. Video/input are untouched.
4. Observe the default output device and output property changes on the HAL
   control queue. Apply read-only master/channel gain and mute to streamed PCM
   before Opus. No HAL queries in the real-time callback or per-frame polling.
   Fixed-volume devices use unity; failed reads mute rather than unexpectedly
   restoring volume. Preserve process ownership and local-speaker suppression.

Validation: portable ring tests including overflow/resumption and concurrent
wraparound; synthetic display tests prohibiting settings reapplication;
synthetic volume tests for master, stereo channels, mute, fixed-volume and read
failure; real lifecycle controller with fake audio boundaries covering restart
cap, teardown barrier, cancellation and denied startup. Run all existing Mac
Host gates and produce a branch-qualified signed/notarized hosted candidate.

Remaining hardware gates: repeated connect/disconnect and sleeping-display
recovery, long-running application/alert audio, mute/volume/unmute and output
device changes, disconnect during recovery, login/logout. Production evidence
and private deployment identities stay outside Git. No live installation is
authorized by the build alone.

Reference: Apple documents that output devices may expose master, channel, or
no volume controls in [QA1016](https://developer.apple.com/library/archive/qa/qa1016/_index.html).
Use current AudioObject APIs, not the deprecated API calls in that old sample.

Candidate status: 1.0.106-macos-media-recovery passed hosted Mac compilation,
all of the above synthetic tests and signed/notarized PKG gates in run
35032611418 at `9af28c1356adada0dc70a1513d80551b37c5479d`. The verified package
is in the local candidate catalog. The operator confirmed volume/mute works,
reported good ongoing behavior, and approved merge/rebuild. No exhaustive
long-duration, output-device-switch or sleeping-display qualification is implied.
